---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/PageHead.astro';
import NextChapter from '../components/NextChapter.astro';
---

<Base title="Local generation and storage" active="local-generation/">
  <PageHead slug="local-generation/">
    New technology is changing how Atlanta makes, stores and manages electricity. Local and distributed energy
    resources reduce dependence on the wider grid and make the whole system more resilient and efficient.
  </PageHead>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Local generation</h2>
        <p>Atlanta gets about <strong>5.2 hours</strong> of peak sunlight a day on average.</p>
        <p>
          Rooftop solar potential across the region is estimated at <strong>4,800 MW</strong>, with more room for
          ground-mounted systems, solar parking canopies and community solar gardens.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Storage and EVs</h2>
        <p>
          Batteries are increasingly installed alongside solar and at key points on the grid. They shave peaks, keep
          the lights on during outages, and shift solar power into the evening when demand is high.
        </p>
        <p>
          More electric vehicles now support two-way charging, so their batteries can power a home or building in an
          emergency.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <h2 class="flush">Why it's needed</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Local generation and storage can soften weather-related outages that hit millions of customers, especially
          in large metro areas like Atlanta. These disruptions are becoming more common as the climate changes.
        </p>
        <h3>What Georgia is already doing</h3>
        <p>
          Georgia has been investing in battery storage and solar generation for the past five to ten years, along
          with technology like two-way power flows that makes decentralized energy more flexible.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure src="lg_texas.png" alt="Photo from the February 2021 Texas winter storm power outage" caption="The 2021 Texas winter outage. Source: NPR" variant="bare" max={460} />
    </div>
    <div class="split">
      <Figure src="lg_bat.png" alt="Utility-scale battery storage installation" caption="Battery storage. Source: Georgia Power" />
      <Figure src="lg_capacity.png" alt="Chart of growth in Georgia's solar and battery capacity" caption="Source: U.S. Energy Information Administration" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section wrap">
    <h2 class="flush">Flexible energy management</h2>
    <Figure src="lg_flexible.png" sizes="(min-width: 1024px) 1100px, 100vw" alt="Diagram of flexible energy management across solar, batteries, EVs and the grid" />
  </section>

  <NextChapter slug="local-generation/" />
</Base>

<style>
  .flush { margin-top: 0; }
</style>
